API vs MCP
===========

An API gives you access to a service.
MCP gives an LLM a standardized way to find and use many services.

---

API
Direct, predefined access to a specific service.

Flow:
  Client (App / Script / LLM)
    -- HTTP request (defined endpoint) -->
  API (e.g. Weather API)
    --> Service (Weather Data)
    <-- structured response

Purpose     Access a specific service or functionality
Interface   Fixed endpoints (REST, GraphQL, etc.)
Scope       One API per service
Discovery   Client must already know what to call and how
Context     Stateless request-response
Example     GET /weather?city=Kolkata  -> weather data

---

MCP
A standard for LLMs to discover and use tools and resources from external systems.

Flow:
  LLM (e.g. ChatGPT)
    -- discover tools & request action -->
  MCP Server
    --> Files (Google Drive)
    --> Code (GitHub)
    --> Databases (PostgreSQL)
    <-- result (natural language or structured data)

Purpose     Let LLMs discover and use tools/resources
Interface   Standardized protocol (MCP)
Scope       Many tools and resources via one server
Discovery   LLM can list available tools and invoke them
Context     Stateful, tool-aware, richer context (files, code, DB)
Example     "List my recent files" -> MCP calls file tool -> result to LLM

